Collectors
----------

Collectors is a utility class in Java 8
that provides methods to collect Stream elements into different results, such as Lists, Sets, Maps, Strings, and grouped data

In simple words:
- Stream processes the data.
- Collectors helps us store or transform the processed data.

Method	             Purpose
toList()	        Collect into a List
toSet()     	    Collect into a Set
toMap()	            Collect into a Map    Collectors.toMap(keyMapper, valueMapper)
joining()   	    Join strings          useful method for string manipulation
groupingBy()	    Group elements
partitioningBy()	Divide into true/false groups
counting()      	Count elements
averagingInt()  	Calculate average
summarizingInt()	Get statistics


toMap() - If duplicate keys exist, This throws IllegalStateException because the key appears twice.
          Use the third parameter: mergeFunction
          Example:
          .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                          name -> name,
                          name -> 1,
                          (oldValue, newValue) -> oldValue + newValue // merge function
                  ))

joining() concatenates the input elements into a single String. It has three overloaded methods:
    1. joining() - Without delimiter - JohnAliceBob
    2. joining(delimiter) - With delimiter(", ") - John, Alice, Bob
    3. joining(delimiter, prefix, suffix) - With delimiter(", ", "[", "]") - [John, Alice, Bob]

partitioningBy() divides elements of a Stream into two groups based on a condition:
        - true → elements that satisfy the condition.
        - false → elements that don't satisfy the condition. It returns a Map<Boolean, List<T>>.
    - Even if one group has no matching elements, partitioningBy() still returns both Boolean keys.

    Example:
    Map<Boolean, List<Integer>> partitioned = numbers.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(num -> num % 2 == 0));
    // This will create a map with two entries: true (even numbers) and false (odd numbers).

mapping() applies a transformation to elements before passing them to another Collector.
    Example:
    List<String> names = Arrays.asList("John", "Alice", "Bob");
    List<Integer> nameLengths = names.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.mapping(
                            String::length,         // Mapper function
                            Collectors.toList()));  // Downstream Collector
    // This will create a list of lengths: [4, 5, 3]

groupingBy() groups stream elements based on a particular property or condition and stores them in a Map - Map<K, List<T>>
    Example:
    List<String> names = Arrays.asList("John", "Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "David");
    Map<Integer, List<String>> groupedByLength = names.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length));
    // This will create a map where the key is the length of the name and the value is a list of names with that length.
    // Result: {3=[Bob], 4=[John], 5=[Alice, David], 7=[Charlie]}

    Variation	                        Purpose
    groupingBy()	                    Groups into Lists
    groupingBy(..., counting())	        Counts elements in each group
    groupingBy(..., mapping())	        Transforms elements in each group
    groupingBy(..., summingInt())	    Calculates sum in each group
    groupingBy(..., averagingInt()) 	Calculates average in each group

summingInt() is a downstream collector that calculates the sum of integer values in each group
